115-He and A. Domrina proved that the Jacobian Conjecture holds true for maps of geometrical degree ≤4 [27,8,7]. Orevkov’s
116-approach uses so-called splice diagrams (introduced by Eisenbud and Neumann [11] in knot theory).
117-   There was an attempt to prove the Jacobian Conjecture by showing topological equisingularity (or C 0 -sufficiency) of the
118-family of curves {f = λ}, see the works of Lê Dũng Tráng, Michel and Weber [16–18]. They also used diagrams to encode the
119-resolution of singularities of the foliation {f = λ} .
120-   It seems that the fundamental problem with the Jacobian Conjecture is the lack of a simple and effective formalism for
121-the investigation of polynomial maps near infinity.
122-   The aim of the present paper is to introduce new tools which unify the algebraic and topological approaches. We
123-define the so-called Newton–Puiseux charts. In the source space the Newton–Puiseux charts are charts near some special
124-divisors obtained after resolution of indeterminacies of P at infinity. On the other hand, they generalize the notion of
125-weighted gradation associated with edges of the Newton diagrams of f and g. Their advantage relies on the simplicity of
126-the Jacobian equation. Moreover, they are not overloaded with abstract formalism, they are only multi-valued changes of
127-coordinates.
